\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {Welcome to Welton\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Apothecary.}
\par\plain {It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a cozy little shop, if by \u8220\'93cozy\u8221\'94 you mean it smells like burnt sage and questionable mushrooms twenty-four hours a day. It\u8217\'92s been in my family for three generations. Passed down like a family heirloom, or maybe more like a family curse. You\u8217\'92d think running an apothecary would be glamorous, all bubbling cauldrons and mysterious concoctions. Let me tell you, it\u8217\'92s mostly inventory sheets and scraping dried bat guano out of glass jars.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\cf1\cb4\highlight4 It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s in the middle of nowhere. Literally. If you walk out the front door, you\u8217\'92ve got three options: Deep Woods, which is basically a labyrinth for mosquitoes; White Cliffs, which are beautiful if you ignore the occasional rockslide; and the Shoreline, which likes to eat tourists who don\u8217\'92t respect the tide. Exciting, right?}
\par\plain {It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s one hour bike ride away from the nearest town Weshaven, and a full-day travel to the nearest city King\u8217\'92s Grave. Which means if you want a pizza delivered, congratulations, it\u8217\'92ll be cold, moldy, and possibly haunted by the time it gets here.}
\par\plain {They say your twenties are supposed to be the \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93best years of your life.\u8221\'94 Whoever said that clearly wasn\u8217\'92t hauling crates of dried mandrake root for their parents\u8217\'92 apothecary at seven in the morning.}
\par\plain {My name\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Edward Welton. Eddie as most people called me, and I just turned twenty last week. What did I get? Cake, sure. A new coat, nice. Oh, and also a new responsibility of managing the apothecary\u8217\'92s delivery to the vendors at Weshaven.}
\par\plain {If this is adulthood, I want a refund.}
\par\plain {But there is one thing that makes this forsaken corner of the world worth waking up to.}
\par\plain {Every morning, I would sneak out of my bedroom window and climb onto the roof. It is dangerous and I could\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve slipped and plummet the kilometres tall cliff to my death. But when you live above an apothecary that smells like roasted lizard skin everyday, you\u8217\'92ll risk a few possible funerals for fresh air.}
\par\plain {The wind\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s different up here. Slower, cooler\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 like it hasn\u8217\'92t yet remembered it\u8217\'92s supposed to be miserable later in the day. It wakes me up better than any cup of coffee. The sky\u8217\'92s still bruised with blue and orange, like the world hasn\u8217\'92t decided whether to stay asleep or get on with it already.}
\par\plain {And then there\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s the sound. The whisper of glass blades brushing against each other in the fields below. Beneath it, miles down, the ocean pounds against the cliffs\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 steady, endless, patient. If you close your eyes, you can almost feel it in your chest.}
\par\plain {Up here, the noise of the apothecary, the orders, the customers, the never-ending smell of dried herbs\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it all fades. For a little while, it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s just me, the sky, and the waves. It\u8217\'92s the only time my head feels\u8230\'85 quiet.}
\par\plain {Of course, the quiet never lasts.}
\par\plain {From below, I heard the faint creak of my bedroom door swinging open. Then, the inevitable.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Edward?\u8221\'94 My mother\u8217\'92s voice, slicing through the morning like a knife through birthday cake.}
\par\plain {I closed my eyes, sighed, and let the ocean fade back into background noise. There it was\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the real tide I lived with: endless waves of chores.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Coming, Mom,\u8221\'94 I called back, already climbing down from the roof tiles. I could guess what this was about. Another early shift. More delivery forms. Sorting through the chicken-scratch handwriting of half our customers just to figure out whether Mrs. Hargreeves needed powdered nightshade or powdered }{\f0\fs24\i1 nightsong.}{ (And yes, those are different. Mix them up, and you get a very apologetic letter from the coroner.)}
\par\plain {I slid back through the attic window, feet hitting the creaky floorboards. The second I shut the window behind me, the ocean and the wind were gone, cut clean off\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 like someone had thrown a blanket over the world. In their place came the house sounds: the endless groan of old wood, the faint rattle of jars on the shelves below, and the chatter of the apothecary staff drifting up through the floorboards.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Goodness, Edward!\u8221\'94 My mother\u8217\'92s voice snapped me upright before I could even dust myself off. She stood at the doorway, arms folded, eyes sharp. \u8220\'93How many times have I told you? No more climbing! You could have slipped!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But I\u8217\'92m okay though,\u8221\'94 I said, brushing my sleeves like that proved it. \u8220\'93See? Not even a scratch.\u8221\'94 Then, after a beat: \u8220\'93...Is something wrong?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Mom sighed and lifted a folded letter in her hand, the wax seal already broken. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I just got an urgent letter from Liese Academy this morning. It\u8217\'92s\u8230\'85 it\u8217\'92s about your sister.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I groaned. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What did Torrie do this time?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You know what she did,\u8221\'94 Mom said, giving me that motherly look that translated to }{\f0\fs24\i1 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8216\'91don\u8217\'92t make me say it out loud.\u8217\'92}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oooh,\u8221\'94 I muttered, dragging the word out.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I want you to go to her school,\u8221\'94 she said firmly. \u8220\'93With the amount of orders today, your father and I can\u8217\'92t leave the shop.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What? Me?\u8221\'94 I jabbed a thumb at my chest. \u8220\'93Couldn\u8217\'92t you just, I don\u8217\'92t know, send a letter back and make Torrie bike home?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m afraid not, Edward,\u8221\'94 Mom said, her tone leaving no room for debate. \u8220\'93They want to talk, so you have to talk to them. You\u8217\'92ve been there before\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it should be easy for you.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Easy. Sure. If by easy she meant the exact opposite.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What about my delivery? Didn\u8217\'92t we have a big order today?\u8221\'94 I tried, already knowing it was a lost cause.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Markus will take over. You need not worry,\u8221\'94 she said briskly. \u8220\'93Just do what I\u8217\'92ve told you.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I sighed, rubbing the back of my neck. No way out, then. A trip back to Liese Academy. It had been a while, but even the thought of setting foot there again made something in me twist.}
\par\plain }